ef 17592186045417] [17592186162214 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186162847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162214 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162214 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f83b8c51-727f-5eb0-9f6f-bcc3b133cfd6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162847 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162847 #edb/ref 1061 30531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162847 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9c19c64f-0597-5273-8ff3-50729d7a67fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162847 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162859 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162859 #edb/ref 1061 30550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162859 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f2c61bc7-19cc-5e6a-a533-0450e49aa5dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162859 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162924 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162924 #edb/ref 1061 31545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162924 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a119eca9-e695-53d9-9049-eef3212a6e22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162924 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162925 #edb/ref 1061 31546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162925 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dd82fa90-8f6a-5c31-a6bd-faa30ff2ee22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162925 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163222 #edb/ref 1064 "A square matrix $A$ is **symmetric** if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$A^T = A$$\n\nEquivalently, an $n\\times n$ matrix is symmetric if $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $1 \\leq i,j\\leq n.$\n\nIf $A$ is symmetric, this really means that it is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n$$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nis symmetric since $A^T = A.$\n\nOn the other hand, the matrix\n\n$$B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nis not symmetric. Indeed, since $b_{12} \\neq b_{21},$ we have $B^T \\neq B.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163222 #edb/ref 1065 4880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163222 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1fe9d8bc-6133-5ab4-8b4f-af2b40817e02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163222 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163254 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $A$ is a symmetric matrix. Then, we have the following properties:\n\n- $-A$ is symmetric\n- $A^m$ is symmetric for any positive integer $m$\n- $A^{-1}$ is symmetric for nonsingular $A$\n\nIf $A$ and $B$ are $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices, we have the following properties:\n\n- $A+B$ is symmetric\n- The product $A\\cdot B$ is *not* always symmetric. For example, if $$A = [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}], \\qquad B = [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$$ then the product $A\\cdot B$ is $$\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\,\\times \\end{aligned}$$ which is *not* symmetric.\n\nFinally, the following two properties can be used to construct symmetric matrices:\n\n- $A + A^T$ is symmetric for any *square* matrix $A$\n- $A^T A$ and $A A^T$ are symmetric for *any* matrix $A$\n\nThe last property is true for *any* $m\\times n$ matrix. This property is particularly useful, as we'll discover in future lessons." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163254 #edb/ref 1065 5043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163254 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c20572b7-1e75-5be2-a39c-b2327caff234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163254 #edb/ref 1067 "Properties of Symmetric Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355333 #edb/ref 1080 "$Q$ and $R$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355333 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355333 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4aed7340-5f0e-5165-ab13-314353711b81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218