\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357611 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "225f8bdf-5da4-5137-973e-d6466e5f7f7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357611 #edb/ref 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358217 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358217 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358217 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "345e70f6-3b7f-5da0-bde7-5d6dee2d58b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358247 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358247 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358247 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3826a4d7-56d7-5e10-a722-00186a58d1cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359178 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359178 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359178 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a417c4ea-e08a-5c5c-9298-aba87be43f9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359200 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359200 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359200 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a7e30533-75d2-5c1b-95fd-97a38042507a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359619 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359619 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359619 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d543ae34-7a48-5fe8-abcf-7b864c0a60f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360437 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360437 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360437 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360437 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360437 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360437 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360437 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360437 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186359178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360437 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f979226a-2f92-55ff-a0a9-14997d804f85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360684 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360684 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96589" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360684 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360684 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{2}$ is always symmetric.\n\nII. The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n\nIII. If $A$ is a symmetric matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360684 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a6c182c5-819c-5206-a031-9309a3557f8d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360684 #edb/ref 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{2}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any non-singular symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{-1})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{-1} \\\\  & ={A}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425147 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186425155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425147 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186425156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425147 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186425157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425147 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425147 #edb/ref 1045 "Symmetric Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425147 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186425156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425147 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425147 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425147 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4147fa8b-5ff8-579d-afae-acd5982a7b2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425148 #edb/ref 1080 "None of the matrices are symmetric" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425148 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425148 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0aa99686-720d-5c83-8792-909de2767703"