ch of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\sqrt{2} & -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425154 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ea940e2f-7cf1-5b69-9241-9cde37e5f341" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425154 #edb/ref 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ and $R$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425155 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186425157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425155 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "13d711fe-4f91-5fa4-8a59-b8df54fc9061" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425155 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186360684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425156 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186425155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425156 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "850fd871-11f1-5a64-9c68-516ffbeed054" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425156 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186357611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425157 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ae65fa76-f15c-5a90-8603-42143fe53ed6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425157 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186425154 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
